I want to create a mobile game, open world pixel style, with simulator crafting and building game about assassin guild manager. This manager's role is as a leader, started from scratch gathering materials to build hideout, recruit assassin, accepting commission, crafting weapon, poison, maintaining food supply with gathering or farming, until he can build assassin castle, recruit legendary assassin, and accepting commission that can change the course of history. The details can be discussed later. If I want to build this game from the scratch, what I need to do first?

1. Lock a one-page design pillar doc (not a full GDD yet)
Just the core loop, the "manager can do it or delegate" pillar, and your 3-5 non-negotiable features. This keeps you from scope-creeping while you build.

2. Build the core loop first, ugly and small
Before art, before content — prototype the systems: gather resource → craft item → assign assassin → timer → collect result. If this loop isn't satisfying with placeholder squares, no amount of pixel art will save it. This is the single highest-risk part of the project; validate it early.

3. Pick your engine/tech stack
For mobile pixel-art with this scope, realistic options:

Unity — most common choice for mobile pixel games, huge asset/plugin ecosystem, easiest to hire help for later if needed
Godot — free, increasingly popular for pixel art 2D, lighter weight, great if solo/small budget
Custom (e.g., web-based/React Native) — only if you have strong programming background already; not recommended for a game this systems-heavy unless you're experienced

Given the game's complexity (inventory, timers, gacha, base building), I'd lean Unity or Godot over anything custom-built.

4. Since you can handle art — build a vertical slice
One hideout, 3-5 resources, 1 craftable item, 1 assignable assassin, 1 commission type, 1 manager. Fully playable loop, ugly but complete. This becomes your proof of concept and pitch tool if you ever want funding/collaborators.

5. Systems architecture before content scaling
Before you make 20 managers or 50 assassins, build the data structure so adding more is just filling in spreadsheets, not rewriting code — item/character data as external files (JSON/ScriptableObjects), not hardcoded.

6. Then, and only then: content, gacha, monetization, polish

